  {
    "episode_id": 66198,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66198.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "I think we have a lot of clients that come out of stressful jobs and things and they retire and a lot of times what they're doing in those first six months to a year is figuring out how to be retired and taking the time to reconnect with their spouse. their grandchildren and figure out what's important to them. Where do I want to spend that at my time? And a lot of times in those first six months to a year they end up spending a little bit more money than they thought because now every day is a Saturday and they can go out to eat whenever they want and go to movies whenever they want. And so as we visit with people and we talk about that transition to retirement and helping them understand you know, where that income is going to come from and some of the things that are going to happen, some of the emotions that are going to happen, helps them transition and helps them help us create a plan that's specific to them that's going to allow them the flexibility to defrag, as you said, or to figure out where they want to be. And then from there, as they live life, we adjust. Right. We adjust, you know, some of them are bored as heck and want to go back to work a little bit and they find something they just love and they work part time. Some of them, you know, dive into charity. Some of them want to travel. And so as we figure those things out, as they really get retired and they're able to experience it. then we're able to help dial that plan and even further and that's a lot of fun. And I think that's kind of what he's talking about is take that time to then now that you have it to figure out what's important to you and how do you accomplish it.",
